WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.240
Look, once you tell everyone your fake prince is&nbsp;
fake, nobody's falling for it again. And how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.501
times can you really watch a cat get rescued&nbsp;
from a tree? Spoiler: the answer is "10."

00:00:11.501 --> 00:00:16.260
Following close behind the premiere of "The&nbsp;
Bachelor," Fox's "Joe Millionaire" is one of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.360
reality dating shows that helped shape the popular&nbsp;
subgenre. It ran for two seasons, with the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.400
season finale being the most-watched entertainment&nbsp;
program in Fox history at the time, as reported by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.200
The New York Times. The second season, titled&nbsp;
"The Next Joe Millionaire" — not so much.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.940
The premise is simple: 20 women compete&nbsp;
for the love of one bachelor. The twist?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.720
They've been fooled into believing the&nbsp;
man is a millionaire when in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.760
he's a construction worker. The idea of lying&nbsp;
to women to then turn around and demean them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.320
as gold diggers brought on well-warranted&nbsp;
criticism towards "Joe Millionaire." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.480
criticism alone will almost never be enough to&nbsp;
kick a show off the air. Instead, it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.180
faulty premise that did this one in. Described&nbsp;
as "a one-time stunt" by the then Fox chief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.620
once the gig was up for "Joe Millionaire,"&nbsp;
there's no more fooling contestants or viewers.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.280
The creators of the show must've&nbsp;
forgotten the only way to build healthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.840
long-lasting relationships is with trust.&nbsp;
Except — not so fast — the show came back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:18.000
in 2022 with its revival titled "Joe&nbsp;
Millionaire: For Richer or Poorer." A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.900
little tweaking to the premise now has the&nbsp;
show featuring 18 women dating two men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.500
not knowing which is a millionaire&nbsp;
and which is not. Over a decade later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.216
"Joe Millionaire" figured out a way to reel&nbsp;
in viewers without lying to its contestants.

00:01:30.216 --> 00:01:36.240
What now feels like a distant fever-dream was the&nbsp;
2010s fascination with home-renovation series that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.980
starred former music sensations, like "Rev Run's&nbsp;
Renovation." Vanilla Ice got in on the popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.540
trend with his own home renovation series "The&nbsp;
Vanilla Ice Project." It may have seemed like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.860
a strange direction for the rapper, but it was&nbsp;
nothing compared to what came next. In "Vanilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.386
Ice Goes Amish, he left the mansions in the&nbsp;
rearview mirror. Well, in a manner of speaking.

00:01:55.386 --> 00:01:57.182
"They don't even have mirrors in their house."

00:01:57.182 --> 00:02:02.040
As much as viewers enjoyed seeing Vanilla Ice get&nbsp;
down and dirty with his handy work, the premise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.780
adjusting to traditional craftsmanship and the&nbsp;
Amish lifestyle wasn't suited for longevity.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.780
The show ran for two seasons from October&nbsp;
12, 2013, to November 15, 2014, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.940
episodes where Vanilla Ice assists a cow in&nbsp;
giving birth and "ices out" an Amish buggy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.560
Mostly, Vanilla Ice would take on renovation&nbsp;
projects within the Amish community. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.720
judging by the show's abrupt and unceremonious&nbsp;
ending, it seems that there simply just weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.050
enough Amish construction projects&nbsp;
that needed a Vanilla makeover.

00:02:31.393 --> 00:02:36.180
Tommy Lee is the epitome of the rock star&nbsp;
lifestyle, so sending him off to college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.120
where many young people learn&nbsp;
about real-life responsibilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.220
is a recipe for peak entertainment.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.220
The Mötley Crüe drummer attended&nbsp;
the University of Nebraska,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:51.060
not to get a degree, but to entertain viewers&nbsp;
at home. From August 16 to September 13, 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.660
Lee filled the shoes of a real-life&nbsp;
student by finding a roommate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.875
trying out for extra circulars, attending&nbsp;
parties, and studying for classes.

00:02:58.875 --> 00:03:05.835
"Uh, Tommy, rad is a measure of angle in physics."

00:03:05.835 --> 00:03:08.940
However, as we're all aware,&nbsp;
college is only temporary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:13.440
leaving the show with little room to grow&nbsp;
and ending after a six-episode run. Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.700
did get to promote his second solo album&nbsp;
"Tommyland: The Ride" by using the album's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:22.746
single "Good Times" as the theme song for&nbsp;
the show. So all-in-all, not a complete flop.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.360
Appealing to a similar music-loving&nbsp;
audience, VH1's "Bands Reunited" may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.780
have bitten off more than they could&nbsp;
chew. The premise of reuniting former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.700
musical groups using elaborate schemes proved&nbsp;
to be more difficult in execution. Turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.504
ex-band members aren't usually keen on reunions&nbsp;
for the sake of a reality show's success.

00:03:40.504 --> 00:03:43.740
"So the question to you is, are you up for it?"

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.282
"No."
"You're not? Oh no!"

00:03:46.282 --> 00:03:51.780
The show managed to run for two seasons from&nbsp;
2004 to 2006, and many viewers witnessed some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.280
of their favorite bands coming back together&nbsp;
to perform past hits. Not every episode was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.120
a success, with either one or all band&nbsp;
members refusing to reunite or perform.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.620
The only way to keep the show going was to&nbsp;
up the antics by going for higher-profile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.880
bands. Their attempt to reunite the legendary&nbsp;
band "The Smiths" resulted in their biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.900
failure yet when they couldn't get the&nbsp;
attention of band member Morrissey. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.200
was a long shot from the beginning, but&nbsp;
these were the shortcomings that left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.125
them with little to go on, and there&nbsp;
was no encore for "Bands Reunited."

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.060
No one could replace Cassandra Peterson as Elvira,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.440
the late-night horror cult movie host from&nbsp;
the 1980s horror movie program, "Elvira's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.700
Movie Macabre." But that didn't stop them from&nbsp;
trying with "The Search for the Next Elvira."

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.260
"Get ready for the most shocking&nbsp;
reality show of all time."

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.300
Running for four episodes on Fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:47.340
contestants dubbed the "unlucky 13" competed in&nbsp;
various challenges, including acting auditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.420
a dinner date with a reality TV star,&nbsp;
and selling Elvira-branded coffee to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.965
tourists. Cassandra Peterson was featured&nbsp;
as a judge along with other guest judges.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.400
"Wait, honey, can I ask you a question?&nbsp;
What is the name of the show?"

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.209
"Mah…macabree?"

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:02.460
"How 'bout macabre."

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.720
"Macabre. Macabre."

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.380
Would you be surprised to learn the series&nbsp;
winner did not, in fact, become the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:13.560
Elvira? Peterson went on to revive her character&nbsp;
in 2010. She returned as a horror hostess in 2014&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.780
with Hulu's "13 Nights of Elvira" and again&nbsp;
in 2021 for a one-night movie marathon on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.480
Shudder titled "Elvira's 40th Anniversary, Very&nbsp;
Scary, Very Special Special." Suffice it to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.980
there was no real search for a new Elvira, making&nbsp;
this series one and done with a single season.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.960
This controversy-ridden reality dating&nbsp;
show had a short-lived premise from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.340
beginning. A group of men and women battle it&nbsp;
out for a chance at romance with social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.080
personality Tila Tequila. The two groups are&nbsp;
unaware of each other and Tila's bisexuality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.340
until the final two contestants are chosen.&nbsp;
Can you think of anything more romantic?

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.700
"I am attracted to each of you&nbsp;
all for very different reasons."

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.800
In theory, the show featured bi-representation&nbsp;
in mainstream media at a time when it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.640
severely lacking. However, tabloids&nbsp;
broadcasted their speculations about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.800
Tila's true sexuality and intentions. In&nbsp;
2007, a source close to the show claimed:

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.543
"Tila has and has had a boyfriend for&nbsp;
over a year, and she's not really bi."

00:06:12.543 --> 00:06:14.820
Tila denied the allegations at the time but later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:19.020
seemingly confirmed the claims in&nbsp;
a now removed 2018 YouTube video.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.920
According to The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;
the reality series was MTV's second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.060
highest-rated series premiere in&nbsp;
2007. But Season 2 didn't live up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.660
to its Season 1 success. "A Shot at&nbsp;
Love with Tila Tequila" proves that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.960
it's difficult to keep a reality dating&nbsp;
show running when your main love interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.950
is rumored to already be in a relationship&nbsp;
and shows no interest in her chosen winner.

00:06:39.356 --> 00:06:45.826
"You could say they're way out of my league,&nbsp;
but for who I'm pretending to be, it's perfect."

00:06:45.826 --> 00:06:50.340
It takes a special kind of someone to genuinely&nbsp;
mix up Matthew Hicks with Prince Harry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:54.000
yet the show "I Wanna Marry 'Harry'"&nbsp;
managed to fool 12 contestants into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.760
thinking they were competing for a&nbsp;
shot at love with the real prince.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.780
And "I Wanna Marry Harry" really went&nbsp;
to extremes to keep the lie alive.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.320
Winner Kimberly Birch recounted&nbsp;
some of the producer's elaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.360
stunts in an interview with Splinter saying,

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.600
"They actually had a therapist come&nbsp;
on set at one point and talk to a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.080
of us who were saying it wasn't him. We&nbsp;
found out later that it wasn't a real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.140
licensed therapist. It was just&nbsp;
someone from the production team."

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.860
In the same vein as "Joe Millionaire,"&nbsp;
once the show aired, there was no chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:23.880
of fooling another set of contestants&nbsp;
or audiences for Season 2. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:27.420
according to Deadline, the series was pulled&nbsp;
from schedule halfway through Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:32.100
dumping the rest of the episodes onto&nbsp;
Fox On Demand and other services.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:37.260
The highly popular MTV reality series following&nbsp;
the lives of a group of good-looking, sun-kissed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:41.340
teens from Laguna Beach High School, "Laguna&nbsp;
Beach: The Real Orange County" was left with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.020
few options for continuing the series once&nbsp;
the original cast graduated. By Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:50.400
the entire cast had been replaced. This didn't&nbsp;
work out as far as ratings were concerned.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.540
Viewers grew connected to the characters&nbsp;
through watching their love triangles, drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:57.384
and the usual stressors of senior year play out.

00:07:57.526 --> 00:07:59.866
"Am I supposed to be really&nbsp;
happy to see you or something?"

00:07:59.866 --> 00:08:03.660
"Uh, no, that's not what I'm saying."

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.320
It was never going to be the same&nbsp;
with a new group of students. Unless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.100
of course, you attempt a spin-off.&nbsp;
Premiering in 2007, "Newport Harbor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.660
The Real Orange County," following the same&nbsp;
format. However, the show unsurprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.186
failed to live up to the success of&nbsp;
"Laguna Beach: The Real Orange County."

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.420
From 2003 to 2012, Mike Rowe graced&nbsp;
our TV screens with the most filthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:29.807
disgusting occupations that go underappreciated&nbsp;
in our society by featuring them on "Dirty Jobs."

00:08:29.807 --> 00:08:35.486
"Ah, see? How did that cow&nbsp;
crap on me three feet away?"

00:08:35.486 --> 00:08:40.380
With his eagerness to get in the mix and try these&nbsp;
jobs out himself, Rowe spotlighted the skills and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.360
tenacity of essential workers from all around&nbsp;
the country. Airing for more than 170 episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.800
it was just a matter of time before the&nbsp;
list of dirty jobs waned thin and sent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:55.980
series into a "permanent hiatus," as Mike Rowe&nbsp;
described it in a 2012 guest column on HuffPost.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.760
"And I've personally done some&nbsp;
things to turkeys and sheep that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.718
I'm not comfortable talking about." Can&nbsp;
we fix the aspect ratio on this please?

00:09:01.718 --> 00:09:06.360
Still, Hollywood's obsession with reboots&nbsp;
has made way for the 2022 revival of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:10.680
"Dirty Jobs." After a ten-year hiatus,&nbsp;
host Mike Rowe admitted to TV Insider,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.620
"The challenge now is not&nbsp;
to get dirty and work hard;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.680
the challenge is to apply it&nbsp;
to jobs that we haven't done."

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.240
After all, you don't just let a five-time&nbsp;
Emmy-nominated series end without a spin-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.140
or revival. In this case, Discovery&nbsp;
did both with the spin-off "Dirty Jobs:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:28.970
Rowe'd Trip" and the 2022 revival of "Dirty Jobs."

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.900
Not all reality shows that have run out of&nbsp;
things to do are complete failures. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:37.800
Animal Planet's "River Monsters" ran&nbsp;
for nine successful seasons. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.980
after years of catching the most&nbsp;
vicious predators lurking in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:44.640
freshwater and saltwater rivers, host&nbsp;
Jeremy Wade revealed in a press release:

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.840
"Some shows can run forever, but our&nbsp;
subject matter is finite. Ten years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.260
I had a list in my head, which seemed&nbsp;
impossibly ambitious at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.840
but everything has now been&nbsp;
ticked off — and then some."

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:59.160
Guided by biologist and extreme&nbsp;
angler Jeremy Wade, the series took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.660
viewers around the globe to explore fearsome&nbsp;
creatures living in the depths of rivers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:08.220
lakes. Both thrilling and educational,&nbsp;
Wade's episodic quests would consist of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:12.240
catching the fish in question and teaching&nbsp;
viewers about the misunderstood specimen.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.000
Fans were disappointed to&nbsp;
see the show go, and surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.200
executives were even more so after losing&nbsp;
one of their best-performing shows.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.820
A reality TV series dedicated to saving&nbsp;
cats stuck in trees. What could possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:28.440
get repetitive about a premise like that?&nbsp;
Airing for a total of 10 episodes in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:33.780
Shaun Sears and Tom Otto of Canopy Cat Rescue&nbsp;
took their rescue efforts to Hollywood with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:37.440
their Animal Planet reality show&nbsp;
titled "Treetop Cat Rescue." Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.920
instead of exclusively watching cat&nbsp;
videos on Facebook, viewers could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:45.480
tune in to half-hour-long episodes starring&nbsp;
our favorite felines in sticky situations.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:50.460
According to The Seattle Times, after the premiere&nbsp;
of the show, the nonprofit rescue team went from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:56.820
saving 250 cats a year to upwards of 750. The&nbsp;
sweet relief of witnessing distressed strays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:01.380
and missing pets meet a happy ending seemingly&nbsp;
would have lasted years on end, but upping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:06.240
ante for television wasn't a viable option for&nbsp;
this serious team of cat rescuers. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.100
one season was all it took to scratch out all&nbsp;
the cat rescue-related ideas. And since there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:16.287
been no revival of this particular series,&nbsp;
we can only assume it's off the air fur-ever.

